00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.470
Listen, nobody actually
likes passwords.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.480
They're hard to
remember, easy to lose,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.300
and don't protect
us all that well.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.220
Not to mention,
people are really

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.040
bad at picking passwords too.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.940
I mean, just look at the most
popular passwords in 2015.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.160
Not very hard to
guess, are they?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.476
Have you used one of these?

00:00:36.476 --> 00:00:38.850
If not, I bet some of the
people you're trying to protect

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.020
are using them right now.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.930
And 2018 doesn't
really get much better.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.060
Maybe we shouldn't
be trusting people

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.700
to keep passwords safe at all.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.700
Turns out they aren't very good
at resisting phishing attempts.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.440
It's especially
easy to get someone

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.840
to fall prey to a phishing
attack with a false sense

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.380
of urgency, such as a fake
"your account is at risk" email.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.470
Then, somebody will
spend less time checking

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.410
for signs of a fake site and
just rush to complete the form.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.820
To help keep you and
your people safe,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.220
Google will stop these
login attempts automatically

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.690
when we notice the
pattern deviates

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.430
from your normal behavior.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.590
But it's not perfect.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.230
This problem is better suited
to a hardware and software

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.850
solution.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.060
And Google has some ideas to
make the password both safer

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.820
and less critical to
the login process.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.670
To start, adding any second
factor is better than none

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.730
at all.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:35.850
Many people use a
one-time password already.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.530
And that is a
security improvement.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.160
But these are still
phishable, especially via SMS.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.860
And the physical fobs that
give you a six-digit code

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.627
are expensive and fragile.

00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:47.460
Besides, if you've seen
that friend carrying

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.860
10 or 20 fobs on a
necklace just to get access

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.260
to the various systems
they use at work,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.880
you can see how
it doesn't scale.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.250
We prefer security key as
a second layer of defense

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.810
in front of your account.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.580
The keys use open
cryptography standards

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.470
similar to public key
encryption and are

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.810
physical devices
like this, which

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.244
you register to your account and
use to prove that you are you.

00:02:08.244 --> 00:02:09.910
When you're logging
in, the security key

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.980
does two important things.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.950
One, it tells a server
that a person actually

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.930
sat at the keyboard
for this login

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.000
because you have to touch
the key to activate it.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.610
So we can stop automated
script-based attacks.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.680
Two, it goes to a challenge
response handshake

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.600
with the server and will
only complete a login

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.410
with the appropriate back end.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.860
So if you go to a cleverly
disguised phishing

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.320
site that tries to steal
your login credentials,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.360
the security key will
fail to authenticate you.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.090
We've mandated security keys
for all Google employees.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.850
And they've shown
tremendous effectiveness

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.040
at preventing
account compromise.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.867
We also see a faster
time to login.

00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:45.450
So we're actually making people
more efficient and more secure

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.040
at the same time.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.560
And we see fewer support
incidents with security keys

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.440
compared to one-time
password fobs.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.130
Lucky for you, the exact same
capabilities can be yours.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.550
G Suite administrators can
enable or require security keys

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.080
for accounts in your domain.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.240
We highly recommend
security keys

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.560
to help you secure your
high-value accounts,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.940
including executives
and domain super admins.

